  <article class="body">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Mru Field</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The supervisor for hard-to-reach systems on Earth. One process on one Linux board runs an operator&#39;s task as
      three replicas and hands every replica the same input. It decides with the{' '}
      <a href="/core/quorum-crate/">
        <code>quorum</code> crate
      </a>{' '}
      and delivers one checked output per cycle.
    </p>
    <div class="row" style="gap: 8px; margin: 8px 0 24px">
      <span class="pill">v0.3.0</span>
      <span class="pill">Rust</span>
      <span class="pill">quorum 0.4.0</span>
      <span class="pill sig">
        <span class="dot"></span>39 unit, 29 integration tests
      </span>
      <span class="pill">26 scenario checks</span>
    </div>

    <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
    <p>
      Field is for systems that run for months between visits: ocean observatories, buoys and moorings, offshore and
      remote sites, polar and seismic stations. On each cycle the supervisor reads one input record from a source the
      operator configures, hands the same bytes to every replica with a shared state record, decides with the{' '}
      <code>quorum</code> crate from mruspace/flight v0.4.0 (unchanged), and delivers one output to a configured sink.
      It retires a replica that is wrong, hung or dead, and goes on with the ones left. It resumes from a checkpoint
      after a reset or a power cut.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Status, November 16, 2026: version 0.3.0, built and tested on the bench.</strong> It runs on
      general-purpose processors (Linux x86_64 and ARM64, macOS, and static ARM builds under emulation in CI). It has
      not run on a board in a system yet, and it has no qualification evidence. The decision rule is the same code that
      ran on ESA&#39;s OPS-SAT PRETTY satellite on October 18, 22 and 28, 2026. The first deployment, an EMSO ERIC ocean
      test, is proposed for 2027. The source is <a href={tree}>mruspace/field</a>; the specification is{' '}
      <a href={`${repo}/docs/spec.md`}>docs/spec.md</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Replicas alive</th>
            <th>Mode</th>
            <th>Delivered when</th>
            <th>Detected, nothing delivered, when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">3</td>
            <td>Vote</td>
            <td>at least two outputs agree</td>
            <td>no two agree</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">2</td>
            <td>Compare</td>
            <td>both agree</td>
            <td>they disagree; the known-answer test names the bad one and it is retired</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">1</td>
            <td>Self-check</td>
            <td>the replica&#39;s two computations agree</td>
            <td>they disagree</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">0</td>
            <td>Halted</td>
            <td>never</td>
            <td>the run has halted and the log says so</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      Spec section 4. The crate decides on a 64-bit FNV-1a hash of each reply (output and next state). The supervisor
      then compares the agreeing replies byte for byte before it delivers.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      A replica that loses a vote gets a strike. Three strikes in 200 cycles start the known-answer test: a pass clears
      the strikes, a fail retires the replica. A replica that does not answer in time, exits, or sends a record over the
      size limit is lost at once. With <code>restart_retired</code> (off by default), a fresh process takes a retired
      slot after a cool-down.
    </p>
    <p>
      Field is not a service manager. systemd restarts a process that dies. Field catches a process that is alive and
      wrong, alive and hung, or restarted from zero after a reset. It runs under systemd and does not replace it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="quickstart">Quick start</h2>
    <p>
      Needs Rust stable. The build fetches the <code>quorum</code> crate from mruspace/flight by tag.
    </p>
    <pre
      class="pre"
      tabindex="0"
    ><span class="k">git</span> clone https://github.com/mruspace/field &amp;&amp; <span class="k">cd</span> field
cargo build --release
target/release/field version
target/release/field check-task tasks/demo/field.conf   <span class="c"># is the task deterministic?</span>
target/release/field run tasks/demo/field.conf          <span class="c"># one cycle per second, until SIGTERM</span></pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      <code>version</code> prints <code>field 0.3.0 (quorum 0.4.0)</code>. <code>check-task</code> prints{' '}
      <code>requests=10000 mismatches=0 lost=0</code> and exits 0. <code>run</code> writes to <code>out/</code> and
      prints the summary when it stops. Stopped with SIGTERM after six cycles:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="pre"
      tabindex="0"
    >field=0.3.0 quorum=0.4.0 policy=shrink task=target/release/field-demo-task adapter=none input=tick output=file:out/delivered
<span class="s">cycles=6 delivered=6 detected=0</span> wrong=- alive=3 mode=vote last_delivered=6 kat_tests=6
stopped=signal at_cycle=6 halted_at=never started=2026-11-18T16:23:04Z written=2026-11-18T16:23:09Z
checkpoint: every=100 resumes=0 last=6 halted=no
footprint: supervisor_max_rss=2048KiB replica_max_rss=1728KiB cpu=0.010s wall=5.6s
limits: memory=128MB/process cpu_share=5% max_run=none reply_timeout=2000ms period=1000ms task_network=deny(not-enforced-here)</pre>
    <p class="tiny" style="margin-top: 10px">
      field 0.3.0 (commit 05c1655) on macOS 27.0.1, Apple M4 (arm64), rustc 1.86.0, November 18, 2026. The footprint
      line varies by host. The network lock is enforced on Linux only, so macOS shows deny(not-enforced-here).
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      The demo task is the flown demo&#39;s payload (a 4 KiB block filled from the input and hashed with FNV-1a) under
      the task interface. The keys <code>tasks/demo/field.conf</code> sets:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="pre"
      tabindex="0"
    >task        = "target/release/field-demo-task"   <span class="c"># the replica executable</span>
input       = "tick"            <span class="c"># the cycle number is the input record</span>
period_ms   = 1000              <span class="c"># one cycle per second</span>
output      = "file:out/delivered"   <span class="c"># cycle, length, bytes, per delivered output</span>
dir         = "out"             <span class="c"># events.csv, summary.txt, checkpoint</span>
kat_input   = "tasks/demo/kat.in"
kat_output  = "tasks/demo/kat.out"
checkpoint_every = 100
summary_every    = 1000
cpu_percent = 5                 <span class="c"># the supervisor&#39;s share of one core</span></pre>

    <h3 id="quickstart-faults">With a fault schedule</h3>
    <p>
      The same task as fast as it answers, 400 cycles, with the bench&#39;s fault schedule and the test-only oracle that
      scores every delivered output. Save this as <code>faults.conf</code> in the repository root:
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">task       = "target/release/field-demo-task"
input      = "tick"
period_ms  = 0
output     = "none"
dir        = "out-faults"
kat_input  = "tasks/demo/kat.in"
kat_output = "tasks/demo/kat.out"
max_cycles = 400
reply_timeout_ms = 200
faults     = "docs/results/2026-10-21-darwin-arm64/faults.txt"   <span class="c"># corrupt:2@30, hang:2@60, stuck:1@120</span>
oracle     = "target/release/field-demo-task"</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      <code>target/release/field run faults.conf</code> prints:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="pre"
      tabindex="0"
    >field=0.3.0 quorum=0.4.0 policy=shrink task=target/release/field-demo-task adapter=none input=tick output=none
cycles=400 <span class="s">delivered=399 detected=1 wrong=0</span> alive=1 mode=selfcheck last_delivered=400 kat_tests=13
stopped=completed at_cycle=400 halted_at=never started=2026-11-18T16:23:24Z written=2026-11-18T16:23:24Z
checkpoint: every=100 resumes=0 last=400 halted=no
footprint: supervisor_max_rss=2128KiB replica_max_rss=1776KiB cpu=0.030s wall=0.3s
limits: memory=128MB/process cpu_share=none max_run=none reply_timeout=200ms period=0ms task_network=deny(not-enforced-here)
faults: injected=3 caught=3 latency_mean=0.0 latency_max=0 (cycles)
fault: corrupt:2@30 caught=30 latency=0 how=outvoted
fault: hang:2@60 caught=60 latency=0 how=lost
fault: stuck:1@120 caught=120 latency=0 how=diagnose</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      And <code>out-faults/events.csv</code>:
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">cycle,event,detail
30,injected,corrupt:2
30,outvoted,2
60,injected,hang:2
60,lost,2
120,injected,stuck:1
120,retired,1 diagnose
120,detected,compare
400,stopped,completed</pre>
    <p class="tiny" style="margin-top: 10px">
      Same build and host, November 18, 2026.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      Replica 2&#39;s flipped bit is outvoted on cycle 30 and the replica stays. Replica 2 stops answering on cycle 60
      and is lost. Replica 1 goes wrong on cycle 120 with two alive: the two disagree, nothing is delivered that cycle,
      the known-answer test names replica 1, and replica 0 self-checks alone from there. 399 of 400 outputs delivered,
      none wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 id="interface">Task interface</h2>
    <p>
      A replica is a process in any language. The supervisor starts it, talks to it over its standard input and standard
      output, and stops it by closing its input. All integers are little-endian. Spec section 3.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Request field</th>
            <th>Size</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">cycle</td>
            <td class="m">8 bytes</td>
            <td>The cycle number, from 1 after a fresh start</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">flags</td>
            <td class="m">1 byte</td>
            <td>Bit 0: self-check. Bit 1: known-answer test.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">input length, input</td>
            <td class="m">4 + n bytes</td>
            <td>The acquired record, the same for every replica</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">state length, state</td>
            <td class="m">4 + m bytes</td>
            <td>The state record from the last delivered cycle; empty on the first cycle of a fresh run</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0" style="margin-top: 16px">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Response field</th>
            <th>Size</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">output length, output</td>
            <td class="m">4 + n bytes</td>
            <td>The output record</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">state length, state</td>
            <td class="m">4 + m bytes</td>
            <td>The next state record</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">second output</td>
            <td class="m">4 + n bytes</td>
            <td>Only when the self-check bit was set: the output of the second computation</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">A length over record_max or state_max makes the replica lost. Framing in src/protocol.rs.</p>
    <ul class="sig-list" style="margin-top: 16px">
      <li>
        <strong>Deterministic.</strong> The same request gives the same bytes. No clock, no random numbers, no reads of
        its own, no state between requests other than the state record it is handed. <code>field check-task</code> tests
        this before the task is supervised.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>On time.</strong> It answers within <code>reply_timeout_ms</code> or it is lost. The first answer after
        a process starts gets 2 s more, once, for the process start. It exits when its standard input closes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Self-check flag.</strong> Compute twice and return both outputs. The supervisor sets it when one replica
        is left.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Known-answer flag.</strong> The request carries the record from <code>kat_input</code>, an empty state
        and the current cycle. The output must equal <code>kat_output</code> and must not depend on the state or the
        cycle. Sent twice at start-up, after three strikes in a window, on a disagreement between two, and every{' '}
        <code>kat_every</code> cycles on the last replica. A wrong or late answer retires the replica.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>
          <code>cli</code> adapter.
        </strong>{' '}
        <code>adapter = "cli"</code> runs a program once per request with the input on its standard input, and reads the
        output to end of file. The state record is handed back unchanged. Slower, and the program cannot keep state, but
        it needs no code change. A fault schedule needs a long-lived process and is refused with this adapter.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="cli">CLI</h2>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">usage: field run CONFIG
       field check-task CONFIG [--requests N] [--seed S] [--inputs FILE]
       field version</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">Any other form prints this text and exits 2.</p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Command</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
            <th>Exit</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">field run CONF</td>
            <td>
              Runs the supervisor until it stops, then prints the summary. It stops on SIGTERM or SIGINT (finish the
              cycle, checkpoint, summary), at <code>max_cycles</code> or <code>max_seconds</code>, at the end of a{' '}
              <code>file:</code> source, or on a halt with <code>on_halt = "exit"</code>.
            </td>
            <td class="m">0 stop, 1 sink or input error, 2 refused configuration or checkpoint</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">field check-task CONF</td>
            <td>
              The determinism test. Starts two instances of <code>task</code>. Each request goes to the first instance
              twice and to the second once. All three answers must be byte-identical. Stops early after three lost
              replies.
            </td>
            <td class="m">0 pass, 1 mismatch or lost reply, 2 task does not start or bad input file</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">field version</td>
            <td>
              Prints <code>field 0.3.0 (quorum 0.4.0)</code>.
            </td>
            <td class="m">0</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0" style="margin-top: 16px">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>check-task option</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--requests N</td>
            <td class="m">10000</td>
            <td>Requests to send</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--seed S</td>
            <td class="m">1</td>
            <td>Seed for the generated inputs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--inputs FILE</td>
            <td class="m">none</td>
            <td>
              Length-prefixed records (4-byte length, then the bytes), one request each, in place of generated inputs
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      Generated inputs: every 10th request is the request number as an 8-byte record, the others 1 to 256 random bytes.
      Every 100th request sets the self-check flag. The state record carries forward from the answers. From
      src/check.rs.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      A task that reads anything but its request fails here. Here the task is a build of the demo task that appends its
      process ID to every output, and <code>nondet.conf</code> is <code>tasks/demo/field.conf</code> with{' '}
      <code>task = "builds/nondet.sh"</code>:
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0"><span class="c"># builds/nondet.sh, executable</span>
#!/bin/sh
exec target/release/field-demo-task --nondeterministic

<span class="c">$ target/release/field check-task nondet.conf; echo "exit=$?"</span>
request 1: answers differ (229 bytes in)
request 2: answers differ (241 bytes in)
request 3: answers differ (78 bytes in)
request 4: answers differ (144 bytes in)
request 5: answers differ (24 bytes in)
<span class="s">requests=10000 mismatches=10000 lost=0</span>
exit=1</pre>
    <p class="tiny" style="margin-top: 10px">
      Same build and host, November 18, 2026.
    </p>

    <h3 id="demo-task">field-demo-task</h3>
    <p>
      The task Field ships with, for the bench, the scenario checks and a self-contained pilot. The output is the 8-byte
      hash; the state is an 8-byte count of the requests answered. With no switch it answers requests until its input
      closes. The <code>task</code> key takes a path with no arguments, so pass a switch through a short shell script,
      as above and as <code>scripts/staged.sh</code> does.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Switch</th>
            <th>Effect</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--once</td>
            <td>
              Read one input record to end of input and write the output, for <code>adapter = "cli"</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--kat DIR</td>
            <td>
              Write <code>kat.in</code> (8 zero bytes) and <code>kat.out</code> to DIR
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--nondeterministic</td>
            <td>Append the process ID to every output; fails check-task</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--echo</td>
            <td>The output is the input record; for the flood bench</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--big-state</td>
            <td>
              Return a 100 KiB state record, over the default <code>state_max</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--bug crash@N</td>
            <td>Exit on cycle N and later</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--bug hang@N</td>
            <td>Stop answering from cycle N</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--bug wrong@N</td>
            <td>Flip one bit of every output from cycle N</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--bug every@M</td>
            <td>Flip one bit when the input&#39;s tick is a multiple of M (a bug only some inputs reach)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--bug kat</td>
            <td>A wrong answer to the known-answer test only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--bug net@N</td>
            <td>
              Right outputs, and from cycle N also 100 UDP datagrams per request to <code>FIELD_DEMO_NET</code>{' '}
              (host:port, default 127.0.0.1:9)
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      Every --bug kind except kat acts on ordinary requests only, never on the known-answer test. The bugs make bad new
      builds for the staged-update and flood tests. From tasks/demo/main.rs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="config">Configuration</h2>
    <p>
      One file, one <code>key = value</code> per line, <code>#</code> comments, strings in double quotes or bare. Every
      key has a default. An unknown key, or a value the supervisor cannot honor, stops <code>field</code> with exit 2,
      so a typo cannot run the defaults. Relative paths are relative to the working directory.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Key</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">task</td>
            <td class="m">/opt/field/task</td>
            <td>The replica executable</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">adapter</td>
            <td class="m">none</td>
            <td>
              <code>none</code> (the request protocol) or <code>cli</code> (a run-once program)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">input</td>
            <td class="m">serial:/dev/ttyS1:9600</td>
            <td>
              The source: <code>tick</code>, <code>file:</code>, <code>serial:</code>, <code>tcp:</code> or{' '}
              <code>cmd:</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">input_backlog</td>
            <td class="m">queue</td>
            <td>
              <code>queue</code> takes every record in order; <code>newest</code> skips what queued up (since 0.3.0)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">period_ms</td>
            <td class="m">1000</td>
            <td>Minimum time between cycles; 0 is as fast as the source</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">output</td>
            <td class="m">file:/var/field/out</td>
            <td>
              The sink: <code>file:</code>, <code>serial:</code>, <code>tcp:</code>, <code>cmd:</code> or{' '}
              <code>none</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">dir</td>
            <td class="m">/var/field</td>
            <td>Where events.csv, summary.txt and checkpoint go; created if missing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">kat_input</td>
            <td class="m">/opt/field/kat.in</td>
            <td>The known-answer test input record</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">kat_output</td>
            <td class="m">/opt/field/kat.out</td>
            <td>Its correct output record</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">replicas</td>
            <td class="m">3</td>
            <td>Fixed by quorum 0.4.0; any other value is refused</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">strikes</td>
            <td class="m">3</td>
            <td>Outvotes in a window that start the known-answer test; fixed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">window</td>
            <td class="m">200</td>
            <td>The strike window in cycles; fixed</td>
          </tr>
