    <h3 id="heartbeat">Heartbeat</h3>
    <p>
      One line every <code>heartbeat_every_s</code> seconds to a file (which holds the latest line only), a serial port
      or a TCP stream:{' '}
      <code>MRU,&lt;cycle&gt;,&lt;mode&gt;,&lt;alive&gt;,&lt;delivered&gt;,&lt;detected&gt;,&lt;resumes&gt;</code>. A
      halted run keeps sending it with the mode <code>halted</code>. A write that runs out of time counts as failed, and
      the run goes on.
    </p>

    <h3 id="checkpoint">Checkpoint and resume</h3>
    <p>
      Field writes <code>checkpoint</code> (one <code>key=value</code> per line) every <code>checkpoint_every</code>{' '}
      cycles, and on every cycle in which a replica was lost, retired, cleared or restarted. It writes a new file, syncs
      it, renames it over the old one and syncs the directory, so a power cut leaves the old checkpoint or the new one.
    </p>
    <p>
      On start with a checkpoint from the same configuration, Field resumes. It cuts <code>events.csv</code> and a file
      sink back to the checkpoint&#39;s lengths, logs <code>resumed</code>, starts replicas for the slots that were
      alive, writes a checkpoint at once, and continues the counts and the cycle number. A retired replica stays
      retired. The cycles after the last checkpoint are lost: their inputs were read once and cannot be replayed. A
      serial or TCP sink has already received those outputs, so cycle numbers can repeat there; a file sink never
      repeats. A halted run keeps its checkpoint with the halt in it. A completed run keeps it too: the same command
      again resumes and stops at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      The quick start run, started again with the same command and stopped after four more cycles, leaves this{' '}
      <code>events.csv</code>:
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">cycle,event,detail
6,stopped,signal
<span class="s">6,resumed,1</span>
10,stopped,signal</pre>
    <p class="tiny" style="margin-top: 10px">
      Same build and host, November 18, 2026.
    </p>

    <h2 id="staged">Staged updates</h2>
    <p>
      Since v0.2.0. A new build of the task is the one change that reaches every replica at once. Three replicas that
      all took the same bad build agree on its wrong answer, or all stop together. With <code>candidate</code> set to
      the new build, Field brings it in one slot at a time and keeps the old build as the check until the last slot
      switches. Spec section 10.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>First trial.</strong> With three replicas alive, slot 2 starts the new build, which must pass the
        known-answer test twice. It then votes against two slots on the old build for <code>candidate_cycles</code>{' '}
        clean cycles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Second trial.</strong> Slot 1 takes the new build for as many cycles again. An output is delivered only
        if the slot left on the old build gave the same bytes, so two slots on the new build cannot outvote it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Last switch.</strong> Slot 0 switches, and the log says <code>updated</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zero strikes.</strong> Until then, one fault of a slot on the new build rejects it: an outvote, a lost
        reply, a failed known-answer test, a cycle with no agreement, or a disagreement with the old build, logged as{' '}
        <code>old-disagrees</code>. Field takes the new build out of every slot and starts the old build there again.
        The same candidate is not tried again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Paused.</strong> If a slot on the old build is lost, the new build comes out of every slot and the
        update waits until three replicas are alive (<code>paused quorum</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resets.</strong> The checkpoint keeps each slot&#39;s build, the trial and a hash of the candidate file,
        so a resume continues the trial. A changed or removed candidate puts every slot back on the old build. To try a
        rejected candidate again, remove it from the configuration for one start.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A run with a build that goes wrong on cycle 150, in the second trial. Save <code>staged.conf</code> in the
      repository root:
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">task       = "target/release/field-demo-task"
candidate  = "builds/new-build.sh"   <span class="c"># exec target/release/field-demo-task --bug wrong@150</span>
candidate_cycles = 100
input      = "tick"
period_ms  = 0
output     = "none"
dir        = "out-staged"
kat_input  = "tasks/demo/kat.in"
kat_output = "tasks/demo/kat.out"
max_cycles = 500
reply_timeout_ms = 200
oracle     = "target/release/field-demo-task"</pre>
