    <h2 id="floods">Flood limits</h2>
    <p>
      Since v0.3.0. Field does not run a network and cannot stop a flood on one. It makes sure a Field node does not
      start one, whatever build of the task it runs, and keeps working when its own source is flooded or its own sink is
      jammed. Spec section 11.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Task network lock.</strong> With <code>task_network = "deny"</code>, the default, Field sets a seccomp
        filter in each task process (the oracle too) between fork and exec. <code>socket</code> fails with EACCES for
        every address family but AF_UNIX. On 32-bit ARM the old <code>socketcall</code> is refused too, and a system
        call from another architecture is refused on every target. Enforced on Linux on x86_64, ARM64 and 32-bit ARM.
        Elsewhere the summary says <code>task_network=deny(not-enforced-here)</code>. If the kernel refuses the filter,
        the task does not start. User-mode emulators refuse seccomp filters, so the scenario checks under emulation set{' '}
        <code>allow</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Input backlog.</strong> With <code>input_backlog = "newest"</code>, after the cycle&#39;s record Field
        reads every complete record already waiting, for at most one period (10 ms when <code>period_ms</code> is 0),
        keeps the newest and skips the rest. It logs <code>source,backlog</code> once when skipping begins and{' '}
        <code>source,caught-up</code> once when it ends. For <code>tcp:</code>, <code>serial:</code> and{' '}
        <code>file:</code> sources. Use it when only the current value matters, such as a sensor reading. Keep{' '}
        <code>queue</code> when every record must be processed, such as a command stream.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Writes with a deadline.</strong> Every write to a TCP peer or a serial port, for outputs and the
        heartbeat, ends at 5 s. An output write that runs out of time stops the run with{' '}
        <code>sink-error tcp: timeout</code> and exit 1, and keeps the checkpoint, so the service manager can start it
        again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Serial lines.</strong> A serial line longer than <code>record_max</code> is cut as it arrives; the rest
        is counted and dropped until its newline. In 0.2.0 a source that never sent a newline grew the supervisor&#39;s
        memory without limit.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A staged update compares outputs, so a build that floods a network while every output is right passes it. The lock
      is what stops that build. The flood bench, with the demo task&#39;s <code>--bug net@500</code> build on 2,000
      cycles of 2 ms:
