    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>One processor.</strong> Three replicas on one processor catch faults in the replicas, not a failure of
        the processor. It is not a hardware-redundancy test. Replicas on a second processor over a link are planned
        (spec section 12); none of that is built.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bench only.</strong> Field has run on general-purpose processors and under emulation, not on a board in
        a system. The power cuts are SIGKILL; a real power cut on a board is not done. Nothing here is qualification
        evidence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Staged updates.</strong> A staged update catches a bad build only while the old build still runs in a
        slot, and only by its outputs. A false rejection is possible: a fault on the one old slot in the second trial
        rejects a good build.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Floods.</strong> Field does not run a network and cannot stop a flood on one. The lock covers the task,
        not the operator&#39;s own <code>cmd:</code> source or sink.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The last replica.</strong> In self-check, a fault that hits both computations alike gets through. Only
        the periodic known-answer test catches a stuck output, after up to <code>kat_every</code> cycles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Compare mode.</strong> Two replicas that disagree and both pass the known-answer test lose that cycle.
        Two that both fail halt the run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fixed constants.</strong> <code>replicas</code>, <code>strikes</code> and <code>window</code> are 3, 3
        and 200, constants of quorum 0.4.0. The configuration refuses other values.
      </li>
    </ul>
